125
00:15:04,160 --> 00:15:07,840
This is a typical laser tube, a helium neon laser.

126
00:15:08,320 --> 00:15:15,840
Basically, the lasers are called the way that they're called because of, if they happen to be a gas laser, of the gas that's inside the actual laser.

127
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:18,720
Argon lasers have argon gas inside.

128
00:15:18,720 --> 00:15:21,440
Krypton lasers have krypton gas.

129
00:15:21,440 --> 00:15:26,080
This is one of the probably one of the most inexpensive lasers that you can purchase.

130
00:15:26,080 --> 00:15:32,960
It's a helium-neon laser, and you can pick up a five milliwatt helium neon laser, which is very low power.

131
00:15:32,960 --> 00:15:36,640
That's five thousandths of one watt.

132
00:15:36,640 --> 00:15:40,000
So picture a one watt light bulb and how dim that is.

133
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:43,600
This is five thousandths of that.

134
00:15:43,600 --> 00:15:46,240
Um you'll notice the outside's made out of glass.

135
00:15:46,240 --> 00:15:47,760
It's a pretty sturdy tube.

136
00:15:47,760 --> 00:15:55,440
You'll see a smaller glass tube running down the law, the larger glass tube, about the size of a drinking straw that you get with a soda.

137
00:15:55,440 --> 00:16:02,280
And it's inside that smaller glass tube where the gas is contained inside of that tube, and the mixture of helium and neon.

138
00:16:02,280 --> 00:16:11,880
And you'll notice, like the gentleman over there, that on the ends of the tube, we have these two little button type things, about this a little bit smaller than the size of MMs, and they're actually mirrors.

139
00:16:11,880 --> 00:16:23,640
And what happens is when we apply power to this tube, that helium-neon tube inside begins to glow just like a neon pizza sign that you see when you go out at night.

140
00:16:23,640 --> 00:16:30,680
And because we have the mirrors on the ends of the tube, what actually happens is the light begins to reflect back and forth between the two mirrors.

141
00:16:30,680 --> 00:16:38,920
It actually bounces back and forth and it amplifies itself, just like when we take sound and we amplify it, or we take power and we amplify it.

142
00:16:38,920 --> 00:16:54,440
And one of the mirrors is close to 100% reflective, or theoretically close enough for me to say 100% reflective, which means when the light comes down and strikes this mirror, 100% of it, or all of it, bounces back up or is reflected back up through the tube again.

143
00:16:54,440 --> 00:16:58,040
Nothing exits out of this end of the tube at all.

144
00:16:58,040 --> 00:17:01,240
The other mirror, on the other hand, is only partially reflective.

145
00:17:01,240 --> 00:17:13,000
We're around 98% reflective, which means when the light bounces from this mirror down to this one, this mirror, excuse me, is only going to reflect 98% of the light back.

146
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:17,880
The other 2% is actually going to come through as the laser beam.

147
00:17:17,880 --> 00:17:29,320
And in a nutshell, outside of the fact that these mirrors have to be placed at an exact multiple of whatever wavelength of light the laser produces, it's basically all there is to it.

148
00:17:29,320 --> 00:17:34,520
There's not a lot to this, as you will see when it gets passed around.

149
00:17:34,520 --> 00:17:44,840
And traditionally with holography, that's the way holograms have always been made with gas lasers or solid-state lasers like Ruby lasers, pulsed Ruby lasers.

150
00:17:45,040 --> 00:17:52,400
If you've ever seen a holographic portrait of someone, chances are it was done using what's called a pulsed Ruby laser.

151
00:17:52,400 --> 00:18:08,640
And this laser is actually a solid state laser, not solid state in the electronic sense, but a solid state that we used to call it because it has a solid ruby tube that runs down, a Ruby crystal rod that runs down the middle of the laser itself.

152
00:18:08,640 --> 00:18:18,960
And around that Ruby crystal rod is a Xeon tube, very similar to the very high power flash tubes that you see photographers use.

153
00:18:18,960 --> 00:18:28,400
And when they flash that Xeon tube, it actually makes the atoms in the Ruby laser throw off photons of light.

154
00:18:28,400 --> 00:18:42,000
And the ends of the Ruby tubes are actually mirrored, just like the mirrors are attached to this one, only the actual Ruby uh rod itself, the ends are mirrored, and the light bounces back and forth and it comes out.

155
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:52,800
It's not a continuous wave laser, it's actually a pulsed laser, and the pulse is very quick, 20 nanoseconds, which is 20 billionths of one second.

156
00:18:52,800 --> 00:19:08,560
And just to give you some sort of an idea how quick that is, if you were to take an F-14 fighter jet going full throttle through the sky, in 20 nanoseconds, that jet would only move forward a fraction of one of its atoms.

157
00:19:08,560 --> 00:19:10,800
That's how fast this flash is.

158
00:19:15,080 --> 00:19:17,480
You can only remember it.

159
00:19:17,480 --> 00:19:24,360
Because by the time the signal goes from the eye to the brain, the flash is already over.

160
00:19:24,360 --> 00:19:28,120
So what you're seeing is the memory of the flash.

161
00:19:28,120 --> 00:19:29,080
It's already over.

162
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:30,200
That's how quick it is.

163
00:19:30,200 --> 00:19:34,040
And essentially what it does is it freezes time.

164
00:19:34,040 --> 00:19:37,080
It literally takes a slice and freezes time.

165
00:19:37,080 --> 00:19:41,960
Because you cannot do live subjects in a hologram traditionally using lasers.

166
00:19:41,960 --> 00:19:51,080
There are some other methods where we can use photographic film or motion picture film first and then transfer that onto what's called a stereogram.

167
00:19:51,080 --> 00:19:56,360
But using lasers, you cannot have any vibration whatsoever in your system.

168
00:19:56,360 --> 00:20:05,400
If anything on your system when you're making a hologram vibrates more than a fraction of a wavelength of light, you have no hologram whatsoever.

169
00:20:05,400 --> 00:20:28,680
And it makes it for a very strange field because if you've ever been in a holography studio to do a hologram, you'll know that the lights go out, heating is turned off, air conditioning is turned off, all sounds, there are no sounds whatsoever, and you stand there and you become very still and quiet before you do the exposure.

170
00:20:28,680 --> 00:20:44,200
It's almost a zen-like experience, because there any type of noise, even just someone talking quietly in the lab, if it's vibrating your eardrum, it's certainly going to vibrate that holographic plate.

171
00:20:44,920 --> 00:20:46,440
Well, how should I put this?

172
00:20:46,440 --> 00:20:47,560
I'll just come right out and say it.

173
00:20:47,560 --> 00:21:08,440
A lot of times F the FBI and other agencies like that, when they want to find out what it is that you're talking about, they can take a laser which operates in the infrared part of the spectrum, which your eye is not sensitive to, and they can shine that beam on your window in your house, or wherever you happen to be.

174
00:21:08,440 --> 00:21:14,360
And as you speak, and as I'm speaking in this room, everything in this room right now is vibrating.

175
00:21:14,360 --> 00:21:19,040
The walls, the floor, the chandeliers, everything is vibrating.

176
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:22,800
And when you speak inside your house, your windows vibrate.

177
00:21:22,800 --> 00:21:33,440
And what happens is when that infrared laser beam that you cannot even see is on your window and you speak, the window actually modulates the laser beam.

178
00:21:33,440 --> 00:21:39,440
So what they do is they read that modulation that's being reflected off of your window.

179
00:21:39,440 --> 00:21:44,160
They can amplify that and send it through an amp and hear what it is that you're saying.

180
00:21:44,160 --> 00:21:48,800
Just like when I speak into the microphone, the diaphragm that's in the microphone is vibrating.

181
00:21:48,800 --> 00:21:50,480
The exact same thing is happening.

182
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:54,800
That's what your window is functioning as a diaphragm.

183
00:21:54,800 --> 00:22:04,000
A little while ago, I had a call from a friend of mine who was going to be a guest on the show named Steve Michael.

184
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:12,560
And Steve Michael lives in Virginia, and he's been involved with holography for many years, back going back to the 60s, actually.

185
00:22:12,560 --> 00:22:27,440
And he said to me, I have a laser pointer, and I've set up a little test with that, and it looks to me as if I could make a hologram with this laser pointer.

186
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:41,520
Now, up until this point, it had always been thought that laser diodes or semiconductor lasers or whatever the term is that you're used to hearing, that you could not make holograms using these diodes.

187
00:22:41,520 --> 00:22:49,120
Holograms have to be made with an instrument like a helium neon laser, which has what's called a long coherence length.

188
00:22:49,120 --> 00:22:54,560
Your coherence length is how long or how deep you can make a hologram.

189
00:22:54,640 --> 00:22:59,440
For instance, you can see a hologram may have six inches of depth or eight inches of depth.

190
00:22:59,440 --> 00:23:04,440
That is due to the fact that the laser has a long coherence length.

191
00:23:04,440 --> 00:23:17,960
With diode lasers, semiconductor lasers, which you find in your laser pointers, it's been always thought that the coherence length is about a millimeter, which is tiny.

192
00:23:17,960 --> 00:23:29,560
If you tried to do a hologram of a coin using a laser diode, you'd find out that you'd be able to get the surface of the coin, but you would not get any depth whatsoever.

193
00:23:29,560 --> 00:23:36,440
But according to Steve Michael's test, he set up something called an interferometer, which can allow you to measure coherence length.

194
00:23:36,440 --> 00:23:39,800
He says, I think I can make a hologram with this.

195
00:23:39,800 --> 00:23:44,520
He said, Let me put off coming on your show until I can test this out.

196
00:23:44,520 --> 00:23:51,320
So he went into his lab and he started working, and he eventually made a hologram using a laser pointer.

197
00:23:51,320 --> 00:23:55,000
I think he paid like $49 for the laser pointer.

198
00:23:55,080 --> 00:23:58,520
He called me and he came on the show and talked about it.

199
00:23:58,520 --> 00:24:09,400
And I went out down to Eckard's drugstore, which is a drugstore in my area, in Allentown area, and I bought a laser pointer for $7.99.

200
00:24:09,400 --> 00:24:21,560
And I brought that into the laboratory and I set it up and I started to work with it, and we managed to get a hologram from the $7.99 laser pointer.

201
00:24:21,560 --> 00:24:24,840
So right away we started to announce this.

202
00:24:24,840 --> 00:24:30,120
And in the scientific community, it's always been thought you cannot do this.

203
00:24:30,120 --> 00:24:34,280
I mean, it's it's what they call it, what did they call it, Sam?

204
00:24:34,280 --> 00:24:36,440
Was it theoretically impossible?

205
00:24:37,240 --> 00:24:41,800
Theoretically impossible to do this.

206
00:24:41,800 --> 00:24:44,120
So what I did is I went out and I bought a bunch of them.

207
00:24:44,120 --> 00:24:46,480
I started sending them around the world.

208
00:24:44,840 --> 00:24:50,800
And I said, I want you people to start doing some holograms with these laser pointers.

209
00:24:51,040 --> 00:24:53,440
And they started turning out beautifully.

210
00:24:53,440 --> 00:24:54,320
Beautiful.

211
00:24:54,320 --> 00:24:55,840
Uh I took one.

212
00:24:55,840 --> 00:25:02,240
Now I did an eight by ten hologram, which is considered a relatively large hologram, eight by ten.

213
00:25:02,240 --> 00:25:05,520
For holograms, uh, eight by ten you're getting up fairly large.

214
00:25:05,520 --> 00:25:10,720
And you usually need a pretty powerful laser to expose an eight by ten hologram.

215
00:25:10,720 --> 00:25:18,080
And I did a beautiful eight by ten hologram of a piece of coral which had about eight inches of depth going back into the hologram itself.

216
00:25:18,080 --> 00:25:20,560
And it was beautifully bright.

217
00:25:20,560 --> 00:25:29,680
So what's transpired over the last few weeks, six or eight weeks, is uh I told Steve, I'm gonna see how far we can take this.

218
00:25:29,680 --> 00:25:43,280
So I started doing multiple color holography, and we started doing a lot of different types of holograms that uh most people wouldn't be able to accomplish with a helium and neon laser uh in a regular laboratory.

219
00:25:43,280 --> 00:25:45,920
And what I want to do, I work a lot with kids.

220
00:25:45,920 --> 00:26:02,800
I've been working with kids maybe about fifteen years or so, started out down at the Franklin Institute in Philadelphia, uh was at Liberty Science Center for a while, which is in North Jersey, and we s wanted to open a science center in the Lehigh Valley, and I got involved with them.

221
00:26:02,800 --> 00:26:10,320
I left the Franklin Institute and Liberty Science Center go and I wanted to s concentrate on helping them get a jump start in the Lehigh Valley.

222
00:26:10,320 --> 00:26:18,000
And I saw this as a wonderful opportunity to have kids get involved making holograms.

223
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:30,280
And uh in fact, my daughter's here tonight, and her physics professor is actually a student that I had in my class back in the early 80s.

224
00:26:29,840 --> 00:26:33,160
And what I do is I don't bring a lot of theory in with the kids.

225
00:26:33,240 --> 00:26:38,920
What I do is I allow them to come in and experience this thing as something fun to do.

226
00:26:38,920 --> 00:26:43,000
We're going to see a little laser light show that they put together for you.

227
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:50,520
I told them I was coming here, and we worked on a Boston Pops piece where they designed all the little laser patterns and stuff for you.

228
00:26:50,520 --> 00:26:55,080
So you'll be able to see that in a few moments as well.

229
00:26:55,080 --> 00:26:59,720
There are still a lot of people that do not believe that it's possible to do this.

230
00:26:59,720 --> 00:27:20,200
I had someone that just drove nine hours down to my studio because he was supposed to do a paper on lasers and holograms, and he decided to do his paper on what I've been doing with holography, and his professor would not accept it because his professor said that it's not possible to do this.

231
00:27:20,200 --> 00:27:23,240
You can't do this in holography.

232
00:27:23,240 --> 00:27:30,440
And what he did is this professor told him that if you can make one of these holograms, then I'll accept the paper.

233
00:27:30,440 --> 00:27:38,120
And what he wanted to do first was set up his own studio, and I told him, you know, it might be better for you just to come down to the lab.

234
00:27:38,120 --> 00:27:40,600
So he came down to the lab and made one.

235
00:27:40,600 --> 00:27:43,880
I had a family come in from the New York area.

236
00:27:43,880 --> 00:27:50,520
They brought their kids, ranged in age from maybe about fifth or sixth grade up into uh high school.

237
00:27:50,520 --> 00:27:52,200
There were five or six kids.

238
00:27:52,200 --> 00:27:56,040
They each made a hologram using this new technology.

239
00:27:56,760 --> 00:28:09,000
And when they went back and they wanted to start doing it on their own, when they called someone to order the materials, the person asked them what is this going to be used for, and when they told him, he told them that you can't do that.

240
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:16,720
In fact, I have someone that's in the audience tonight that came to see me who's from a rather large research facility.

241
00:28:16,720 --> 00:28:18,720
He's from Bell Labs.

242
00:28:18,720 --> 00:28:22,960
And you saw you saw the work on the web, right?

243
00:28:22,960 --> 00:28:29,840
And when you when you spoke about this to some of your fellow researchers, what did they say about it?

244
00:28:29,840 --> 00:28:31,520
It was impossible.

245
00:28:33,040 --> 00:28:36,560
So you came from Bell Labs and he came to my studio.

246
00:28:36,560 --> 00:28:38,640
And what did you find out?

247
00:28:41,360 --> 00:28:42,160
Yeah.

248
00:28:42,160 --> 00:28:45,120
So this is this is a whole new field of holography now.

249
00:28:45,200 --> 00:28:48,560
There's there's several things that this is going to allow us to do.

250
00:28:48,560 --> 00:28:54,400
Number one, it's going to allow young students to get involved with holography.

251
00:28:54,400 --> 00:29:02,400
The hologram that I show you of the Kennedy assassination bullets was made on a system, not just a laser, but a system.

252
00:29:02,400 --> 00:29:11,120
The entire laser holography optical system cost thirty dollars.

253
00:29:11,120 --> 00:29:13,040
Four by five inch, beautiful.

254
00:29:13,040 --> 00:29:16,720
Now, these aren't making little holograms that you'd be ashamed to show someone.

255
00:29:16,720 --> 00:29:19,840
These are exhibit quality holograms.

256
00:29:19,840 --> 00:29:26,560
The entire optical system, including the laser, $30.

257
00:29:26,560 --> 00:29:30,800
Also, for artists who want to get involved in the field.

258
00:29:30,800 --> 00:29:34,000
They like to work, bless you, they like to work with bigger pieces.

259
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:37,520
They don't like small pieces, they like to create big holograms.

260
00:29:37,520 --> 00:29:51,600
Sam and I are looking into working with very high power diodes where we can create large works that used to cost maybe about $10,000 to create for one of them.

261
00:29:52,800 --> 00:29:56,560
We think we can get that price down quite a bit to do this type of work.

262
00:29:56,560 --> 00:29:57,920
So it's going to open it up.

263
00:29:57,920 --> 00:30:00,840
It's going to it's going to make a lot of changes in holography.

264
00:30:00,840 --> 00:30:07,080
And I don't think that the laser manufacturers are probably going to be very happy about it.

265
00:30:07,320 --> 00:30:12,120
For instance, I have a what's called a 10 milliwatt laser.

266
00:30:12,120 --> 00:30:25,640
In a science catalog, which some of you may be familiar with, it costs $1,650 for a 10 milliwatt laser.

267
00:30:25,640 --> 00:30:29,880
You can get a 10 milliwatt diode.

